54 contact points and alternate prey availability, and (3) upgrading ecosystem models to evaluate
55 alternative ecosystem management strategies. Importantly, considering additive predation
56 impacts due to predator behaviors (e.g., predators moving inshore, upstream) and foraging
57 responses (i.e., Holling's functional and numerical responses) should be part of management
58 evaluations as these processes control the potential impacts of interactions with salmon at
59 contact points modulated by salmon growth and alternate prey availability. Key objectives for
60 future research include identifying connections with predator populations and their community
61 spatiotemporal patterns of abundance and distribution, and understanding environmental
62 influences on predator-salmon interactions.},
	journal = {Ices Journal of Marine Science},
	author = {Wells, Brian K. and Huff, David D. and Quinn, Thomas P. and Santora, Jarrod A. and Gomes, Dylan G. E. and Vasbinder, Kelly and Barnas, Katie A. and Burke, Brian J. and Courtney, Michael B. and Crozier, Lisa G. and Fiechter, Jerome and Friedland, Kevin D. and Hayes, Sean A. and Hunsicker, Mary E. and Juanes, Francis and Mantua, Nathan J. and Michel, Cyril J. and Phillips, Elizabeth M. and Sanderson, Beth L. and Satterthwaite, William H and Seitz, Andrew C. and Sheehan, Timothy F. and Stewart, Joshua D. and Trudel, Marc},
	year = {2025},
